  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1234 AM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nThe upper-level synoptic pattern is made up of two key features. \nThe first is a shortwave trough centered over eastern Arkansas and a \ndeepening trough positioned over the Pacific Northwest. Nearer to \nthe surface, high pressure sits over the southern Rockies and low \npressure among the northern Rockies. Sunday will be a near carbon \ncopy of Saturday albeit a couple degrees warmer. Dry with a strong \nsouth/southwest wind at around 25 mph and gusts up to 35 mph. Highs \nagain will be in the mid to upper 80s and potentially around 90 \ndegrees in the warmest areas keeping up the pattern of warmer than \nnormal highs. Monday will again be similar with weaker winds and \nwarmer highs. With highs in the low 90s and the southwest winds only \naround 20 mph and gusts to 30 mph. Relative humidity minimums are \nforecast to stay above 25% to quell most of the fire weather risk. \n\nThe interest of the forecast period surrounds the system currently \nsituated over the Pacific Northwest. With a bunch of moisture being \nadvected into Kansas prior to the system with the addition to some \ntropical system moisture again, widespread storms/rain is possible \nthrough Wednesday. Tremendous uncertainty is still present in both \nthe system and cold front timing. Trends continue to depict both as \nslower than initially progged. Despite those questions and the \ntemporal uncertainty, it appears like there remains a notable chance \nfor severe weather. The SPC has a 15% box along the KS/OK border. \nThe CSU MLP has a 5-15% both Tuesday and Wednesday in the eastern \nzones. Early GFS forecast soundings has CAPE around 1000 J/kg and \nshear of around 30 KTs. This is a marginally conducive environment \nfore severe weather so the potential will need to be closely \nmonitored. Through the couple rounds of precipitation Tuesday and \nWednesday, ensembles have the early amount indications of 0.5-1.5\" \nof rain with the drier regions out west and the wettest zones near \nsouth central Kansas. This is trending much wetter than previous \nruns and will need to be monitored. If the system track continues to \nbe later than depicted, these amounts may drop or shift eastward. \n\nOther than the precipitation, it will obviously be much cooler \nbehind the cold front. Wednesday and Thursday are forecast to have \nhighs in the 70 and highs dropping even lower Friday and Saturday \ntowards the 60s. It will need to be monitored the timing of the\nfront and the CAA behind it.",
